#
# config.table - what each parameter of sd.conf is, for TOOL.CONFIG (S.75 A6).  One line to a parameter, eight fields separated by a vertical bar:
#
#   key | kind | default | min | max | effect | status | text
#
#   key      the name as sd.conf spells it (gplsrc/config.c read_config() is the list; test-toolconfig-units.py holds this table to it)
#   kind     int, bits (flag bits added together), flag (0 or 1), text, path, float, pair (base,range) or triple (port,user,range)
#   default  what SD uses when sd.conf does not say (the value config.c sets before it reads the file); the shipped sd.conf says more for a few
#   min,max  the range config.c refuses outside of (rangecheck), blank when there is none
#   effect   session: a session reads sd.conf itself when it starts, so an edit reaches the sessions started after it; restart: it sizes or starts the system, so it takes
#            effect when SD is next started; now: a program reads sd.conf each time it uses the parameter, so a change needs no restart and no new session
#   status  live (something acts on it), inert (accepted and stored, nothing acts on it), retired (accepted and ignored so that an old file still starts), file (read from
#            sd.conf by a program each time it is used, not by the kernel)
#   text     one sentence, plain, no vertical bar
#
# A line beginning with # is a comment.  Alphabetical by key.
#
APILOGIN|flag|1|||restart|retired|Retired on 14 September 2026: the API always logs in by SCRAM now. The line is still accepted and ignored so that an sd.conf written earlier still starts SD.
BACKUPDIR|path||||now|file|The directory set.backup.directory saves. The kernel ignores it; the backup and restore programs read sd.conf each time they run, so a change needs no restart.
CLEANUP|int|300|30|3600|restart|live|Seconds between the checks that look for sessions that ended without signing off (a killed terminal, a lost connection) and clear them from the user table.
CMDSTACK|int|99|||restart|live|How many commands each session keeps on its command stack.
CODEPAGE|int|0|||session|inert|Stored and readable, and nothing acts on it.
CREATUSR|flag|1|||session|retired|SD has accounts rather than accounts and users, so create.account always makes the operating system account; the value is read and discarded.
DEADLOCK|int|0|0|2|restart|live|0 does nothing special; 1 makes SD trap a deadlock between sessions; 2 does that and also writes a file deadlock.n with the locks involved.
DEBUG|bits|0|||restart|live|Flag bits that turn on debugging features of the kernel. Leave at 0 unless asked to change it.
DHCACHE|int|10|0|1024|session|live|How many closed dynamic files SD keeps open for the next use, so that opening them again is quick.
DUMPDIR|path||||session|live|The directory where a process dump is written. The shipped file names the dumps directory of the system account.
ERRLOG|int|0|||restart|live|The size of the error log in kilobytes; a value below 10 is raised to 10 and the oldest entries are discarded when it is full. 0 keeps no limit.
EXCLREM|flag|0|||session|inert|Stored and readable, and nothing reads it. It described leaving remote files out of an account save.
FDS|int|32767|||restart|live|A limit on the file descriptors SD uses; when it is not set there is no limit of SD's own.
FILERULE|bits|0|||session|live|Which special forms of file name in a VOC entry are honoured: 1 account:file, 2 system:account:file (opens nothing on this port), 4 path: followed by a pathname.
FIXUSERS|pair||||restart|live|base,range: user numbers kept for sessions that ask for one number, such as a service that must always be the same user.
FLTDIFF|float|0.0000000000291|||session|live|Two floating point numbers closer together than this compare equal.
FSYNC|bits|0|||session|live|Flag bits that say when SD forces data to disk with fsync: a higher value is safer and slower.
GDI|flag|0|||session|inert|Selects the Windows printing interface; nothing on this port acts on it.
GRPDIR|path|/home/sd/group_accounts|||session|live|The directory create.account puts a group account in.
GRPSIZE|int|4|1|8|session|live|The group size, in kilobytes, of a hashed file made without one. 4 since 25 October 2026; an installed system keeps the value its sd.conf has until you change it.
INTPREC|int|11|0|14|session|live|How many digits of precision integer arithmetic and the INT function keep.
INVCASE|flag|0|||session|live|1 starts every session with the terminal's input case inverted.
JNLDIR|path||||restart|inert|The journal directory. SD has no journalling, so nothing reads it.
JNLMODE|int|0|||restart|inert|The journalling mode. SD has no journalling, so nothing reads it.
LGNWAIT|int|0|0|32767|restart|live|Seconds a sign-on waits for a free user number when the user limit is reached; 0 refuses at once.
LPTRHIGH|int|66|10|32767|session|live|The default page depth of the printer, in lines.
LPTRWIDE|int|80|10|1000|session|live|The default page width of the printer, in characters.
MAXCALL|int|10000|10|1000000|session|live|The deepest nesting of subroutine calls one program may reach.
MAXIDLEN|int|63|63|255|restart|live|The longest record id. 63 is also the lowest value allowed.
MAXRLOCK|int|0|0|100000|session|live|How many record locks one process may hold; 0 sets no limit.
MUSTLOCK|flag|0|||session|live|1 makes a write or delete fail unless the record is locked first.
NETFILES|bits|0|||restart|live|Bit 2 gates one old API request (number 29); SDNet does not read it. Leave it at 0.
NETREPLY|int|30|1|3600|session|live|Seconds to wait for each reply from another SD server once connected; a slower server is treated as lost.
NETWAIT|int|5|1|3600|session|live|Seconds to wait for a connection to another SD server, for the TCP connect and again for the TLS handshake. 0 is refused.
NUMFILES|int|80|||restart|live|How many files may be open across all sessions.
NUMLOCKS|int|100|1|32767|restart|live|How many record locks may be held across all sessions.
NUMUSERS|int|1|||restart|live|How many sessions may run at once; it sizes the user table in shared memory. The shipped file says 20.
OBJECTS|int|0|||session|live|How many compiled programs one session keeps in memory at once; 0 sets no limit.
OBJMEM|int|0|||session|live|How much memory, in kilobytes, those programs may take; 0 sets no limit.
PDUMP|bits|0|||restart|live|Flag bits that say when a process dump is written.
PHANTOMS|pair||||restart|live|base,range: user numbers kept for phantoms started with phantom user n. Nobody else is given one.
PORTMAP|triple||||restart|live|base_port,base_user,range: gives a connection a fixed user number derived from the port it arrived on.
PWDELAY|int|3|0|3600|session|live|Seconds a failed network sign-on is made to wait before the refusal is sent.
RECCACHE|int|0|0|32|session|live|How many records each file keeps cached for the next read.
RINGWAIT|flag|1|||session|inert|Stored and readable, and nothing acts on it.
SAFEDIR|flag|0|||session|live|1 updates directory files by writing a new file and renaming it, rather than in place.
SCREENTHEME|text||||session|live|The colour theme a program using the SCREEN statement gets when it does not choose one: mono, classic or blue. Not readable with the CONFIG function.
SDCLIENT|bits|0|||session|live|Restricts what an API session may do. Any non-zero value stops files being opened, and 2 also refuses subroutines not compiled as callable.
SDSYS|path||||restart|live|The system account's directory. SD does not start if the global catalogue is not found beneath it. Not readable with the CONFIG function.
SH|text||||session|live|The command a bare sh starts as an interactive shell; when it is not set SD uses /bin/bash -i.
SH1|text||||session|live|The command sh followed by a command line uses; when it is not set SD uses /bin/bash -c.
SORTMEM|int|4096|||session|live|Kilobytes of data above which a sort works on disk instead of in memory.
SORTMRG|int|4|2|10|session|live|How many work files a disk sort merges at once.
SORTWORK|path||||session|live|The directory for the work files of a sort that does not fit in memory; when it is not set SD uses the system account's directory.
SPOOLER|text||||session|live|The spooler print jobs are handed to by default.
STARTUP|text||||restart|live|A command SD runs when it starts.
TEMPDIR|path||||session|live|The directory for temporary files; when it is not set SD uses the system default.
TERMINFO|path||||session|live|An extra directory of terminfo definitions. The shipped definitions are found without it.
TXCHAR|flag|1|||session|inert|Stored and not readable: nothing acts on it on this port.
USERPOOL|int|1023|0|16383|restart|live|The highest user number handed out; user numbers rotate through 1 to this value.
USPLIT|flag|0|0|1|restart|live|1 gives interactive sessions the lowest free user numbers and every other process (a phantom, an API session) the highest.
USRDIR|path|/home/sd/user_accounts|||session|live|The directory create.account puts a user account in.
YEARBASE|int|1930|||session|live|The base year of a two-digit year: with 1930 the two-digit years run from 1930 to 2029.
